  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\n18Z surface analysis shows high pressure centered over the east \ncoast of the US with low pressure over the Oklahoma panhandle. A \nribbon of both Pacific and Gulf moisture extends north from the low \ninto parts of of the Middle Mississippi Valley and Great Lakes \nRegion. This is collocated with a 30-40 kt LLJ and has resulted\nin a large area of warm-air advection steady rain showers over \na good portion of the central US, including north-central \nIllinois. Radar estimates show the highest precipitation amounts\nhave fallen north of the Illinois River Valley, where is \npainting approximately 0.5- 1.5\". A lull in the precipitation is\nanticipated this evening and overnight as mid-level heights \nrise with a small area of ridging. Lingering cloud cover will \nkeep overnight temperatures mild with forecast lows expected to \nbe in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\nA closed upper low over the south-central Plains will become an open \nwave on Thursday as upper ridging builds over the southwest US. At \nthe surface, a cold front will approach from the northwest, spilling \nthe tropical moisture further southeast through the area and \nbringing another round of showers and storms Thursday into Friday, \nthis time more widespread. Climatologically high PWATS and deep warm-\ncloud layers will bring pockets of moderate to heavy rainfall. The \n30.12Z HREF 24 hour QPF localized probability-matched mean (LPMM) \nending at 12Z Friday shows several narrow bands of 2 to 3\"+ of rain \nacross central Illinois. Although we can take some rain, localized \nflooding could become an issue especially if the rain falls over \nareas that received the heavier rainfall amounts today. \nPrecipitation will come to an end by Friday afternoon as the front \nquickly pushes south of here.\n\nHigh pressure settles into the Midwest states behind the cold front \nlate Friday into Saturday, bringing a period of cooler and drier \nweather through early next week. High temperatures look to top \nout in the upper 60s to low 70s with overnight lows in the 40s.",
